# ================================================================== PART 1 · THE PARTS
flow(id="jobs", part=0, scope="world",
 title="The Four Jobs of a Financial System",
 kick="Every bank, fund, exchange and insurer on earth does one or more of four jobs with money that belongs to someone else.",
 sum="This map is about the middle of finance: the banks, insurers, pension funds, fund managers, exchanges and payment systems that most people mean when they say the financial system. It leaves out how money is created, what central banks do, the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank, tax havens and world trade. Those have their own pages. What is left is the largest part by far, and the part every household uses whether it wants to or not.<br><br>The first eight chapters make no argument. They set out the parts and then the motion, with small numbers, so that the later chapters can be read without stopping to look words up. I chose that order because the second half of the map is about who controls these parts and what they charge, and that only makes sense to someone who already knows what each part is for.<br><br>A financial system does four jobs. It moves money from a payer to a receiver. It keeps money and investments safe and keeps the record of who owns them. It passes the savings of people who have spare money to people, companies and governments who want to borrow. And it spreads risk, so that a house fire or an early death does not ruin one family. Nearly all of the money involved belongs to the customers and not to the firms that handle it.<br><br><b>How to move through the map:</b> press Play the story to begin. The buttons along the bottom pause, go back or forward one slide, and jump between chapters. Any box can be closed with its ×.",
 bridge="The three slides start from one village and widen out. The first shows the four jobs with one saver and one borrower. The second counts what the world's financial firms hold today and shows how large the banks are next to each country's economy. The third shows the two routes savings can take to a borrower, through a bank or through a market.",
 out="Four jobs, two routes, and about 500 trillion dollars that mostly belongs to customers. The next chapter meets the firms that take the money in.",
 rec="A financial system moves money, keeps it safe, passes savings to borrowers and spreads risk, and it does this with money that belongs to its customers.",
 roles=[],
 src=[S("Financial Stability Board, Global Monitoring Report on Nonbank Financial Intermediation 2025", FSBR),
      S("World Bank, Global Financial Development Database: deposit money banks' assets to GDP", WBI + "GFDD.DI.02"),
      S("World Bank, Market capitalization of listed domestic companies (per cent of GDP)", WBI + "CM.MKT.LCAP.GD.ZS")],
 steps=[
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "Four Jobs, Done With Somebody Else's Money",
   "Picture a village with one baker and one farmer. The baker has 100 coins she does not need this year. The farmer needs 100 coins for seed. A go-between takes the baker's coins, promises her 102 back next year, lends them to the farmer and asks him for 106. The go-between keeps 4. That gap is called a spread, and it is the first of the two ways finance is paid. The second way is a fee: a fixed charge for moving the coins, guarding them or keeping the record. Every institution on this map is paid by a spread, a fee or both. Notice what the go-between risked. The coins were the baker's. If the farmer's crop fails, the question of who loses, the baker or the go-between, is the question the last part of this map is about.",
   FSBR, w=1, note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because it explains an idea that applies everywhere. The village and its numbers are a worked example I wrote to show the rule."),
  st("2021", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "How Large the Banks Are Next to the Economy",
   "The Financial Stability Board, a body of finance ministries and central banks, counts what financial firms hold in 29 economies that produce over 90 per cent of world output. At the end of 2024 the total was about 500 trillion dollars, close to five times what the world produces in a year. A little under half sat in banks and central banks. The rest, 256.8 trillion, sat in firms that are not banks: pension funds held 44.1 trillion, insurers 38.9 trillion, and investment funds most of the remainder. The map shades each country by the assets of its banks as a share of its yearly output, known as GDP. Among the %(bankassets_n)s countries with a figure, banks in %(bankassets_100)s hold more than the country produces in a year. In those countries a mistake by the banks is larger than anything the government could easily cover.",
   WBI + "GFDD.DI.02", data={"k": "bankassets"}),
  st("Latest year", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "Two Routes: Through a Bank or Through a Market",
   "Savings can reach a borrower by two routes. On the first, a bank takes deposits and makes loans, and the loan stays on the bank's books. On the second, the borrower sells a piece of paper directly to investors: a bond, which is a loan cut into tradable pieces, or a share, which is a slice of ownership in a company. Then nobody lends. Investors buy, and other firms arrange the sale, run the market and keep the records. If a country relies on the first route, its banks are the system. If it relies on the second, fund managers, exchanges and the firms behind them matter as much. The map shades each country by the value of its listed companies next to its GDP, a rough measure of how much runs through markets. %(mcap_hi)s of %(mcap_n)s countries are above 100 per cent, the United States among them.",
   WBI + "CM.MKT.LCAP.GD.ZS", data={"k": "mcap"}),
 ])

flow(id="takers", part=0, scope="world",
 title="The Firms That Take Your Money In",
 kick="Three kinds of institution collect money from households: banks, insurers and pension funds. Each makes a different promise in return.",
 sum="Most people meet the financial system at three counters. At the first they hand over wages and are promised the money back whenever they ask. That is a bank. At the second they pay a small sum every month and are promised a large sum if something goes wrong. That is an insurer. At the third, money is taken from their pay for forty years and they are promised an income when they stop working. That is a pension fund.<br><br>The promise decides everything else about the firm. A bank that must repay on demand behaves differently from a fund that owes nothing for decades. The promise also decides what can go wrong. For each of the three, this chapter gives the promise, the way the firm earns its living, and one rule in the form of if this, then that. Later chapters lean on those rules.",
 bridge="The four slides take the counters in turn. The first follows a deposit into a bank and out again as a loan, and maps the margin banks keep in each country. The second explains the insurer and the pool of money it holds while it waits for claims. The third explains the pension fund. The fourth shows what the three have in common: none of them keeps your money in a vault.",
 out="Banks owe money on demand, insurers owe it if something happens, pension funds owe it decades from now. All three pass most of it on to someone else to invest, and the next chapter meets those firms.",
 rec="Banks, insurers and pension funds each take money in against a promise, and each passes most of it on.",
 roles=[],
 src=[S("World Bank, Global Financial Development Database: bank net interest margin", WBI + "GFDD.EI.01"),
      S("World Bank, Global Financial Development Database: insurance company assets to GDP", WBI + "GFDD.DI.11"),
      S("World Bank, Global Financial Development Database: pension fund assets to GDP", WBI + "GFDD.DI.13"),
      S("Financial Stability Board, Global Monitoring Report on Nonbank Financial Intermediation 2025", FSBR)],
 steps=[
  st("2021", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "The Bank: Borrow Short, Lend Long, Keep the Gap",
   "A commercial bank is the kind with branches and cash machines. It takes deposits, which it must repay whenever the customer asks, and makes loans that are repaid over years. Suppose it pays depositors 1 per cent and charges borrowers 5. The gap, after the cost of running the bank, is called the net interest margin. A savings bank, a cooperative bank and a credit union do the same job; the difference is that their customers own them. Now the rule. If every depositor asks for their money on the same day, the bank cannot pay, because the money is out on loan. That is a bank run, and it can sink a bank that has made no bad loans at all. The map shades the margin banks keep in each country. In %(nim_hi)s of %(nim_n)s countries it is 5 per cent or more. A wide margin means savers are paid little, borrowers are charged a lot, or both.",
   WBI + "GFDD.EI.01", data={"k": "nim"}),
  st("2020", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "The Insurer: Paid Now, Pays Later",
   "An insurer collects a premium from many people and pays out to the few who suffer the loss. A thousand households pay 500 each against fire. Three houses burn, and the insurer pays for them out of the 500,000. A life insurer does the same over decades. The premiums arrive long before the claims, so an insurer always holds a large pool of other people's money. The industry calls it the float, and the insurer invests it, mostly in bonds. A reinsurer is an insurer for insurers: it takes a share of the largest risks, such as earthquakes, for a share of the premium. The rule: if claims arrive faster or larger than the insurer assumed, or its investments fall, the pool may not cover what was promised. Insurers in the 29 economies the Financial Stability Board counts held 38.9 trillion dollars at the end of 2024. The map shows their assets next to each country's GDP.",
   WBI + "GFDD.DI.11", data={"k": "insassets"}),
  st("2020", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "The Pension Fund: Forty Years of Somebody's Wages",
   "A pension fund collects part of a worker's pay for decades and pays an income in retirement. There are two kinds. In the first the fund promises a set income, and the employer must make up any gap. In the second the worker simply gets whatever the savings have grown to, so the worker carries the risk. Most new schemes are of the second kind. A pension fund owes nothing for years, so it can hold investments a bank could not. But few funds invest the money themselves. They hire asset managers, on the advice of consultants, and each takes a fee. The rule: if the investments earn 1 per cent a year less than expected, for forty years, the pension at the end is roughly a quarter smaller. Pension funds held 44.1 trillion dollars at the end of 2024. The map shows where that money is: %(pension_hi)s of %(pension_n)s countries with a figure have pension assets above half their GDP, and most have very little.",
   WBI + "GFDD.DI.13", data={"k": "pension"}),
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "None of Them Keeps Your Money in a Vault",
   "The three counters have one thing in common. The money does not stay where you put it. A bank turns your deposit into someone's mortgage. An insurer turns your premium into bonds. A pension fund hands your savings to a manager who buys shares. What you hold afterwards is a claim: a promise from the institution, written in its records. This is the reason the system needs everything in the next chapter. Someone has to choose the investments, someone has to arrange the sale of the bonds and shares, someone has to run the market where they change hands, someone has to hold them, and someone has to say whether they are safe. Each of those jobs is now a separate industry, and each is paid from the same savings.",
   FSBR, w=1, pl=["jpm", "icbc", "allianz", "pingan", "gpif", "nbim", "abp", "calpers"], note="The ringed circles are examples of the three kinds of institution in this chapter: two banks, two insurers and four pension or state funds, each at its headquarters. They are examples, not a complete list; the full directory is in the legend."),
 ])

flow(id="middle", part=0, scope="world",
 title="The Firms That Work in the Middle",
 kick="Between the saver and the borrower stand six more kinds of firm. Most people never deal with them directly, and all of them are paid from the saver's money.",
 sum="The last chapter ended with savings leaving the bank, the insurer and the pension fund. This chapter follows them into the middle of the system. Six kinds of firm work there. The asset manager chooses what to buy. The investment bank creates the bonds and shares and sells them. The broker, the exchange and the market maker let them change hands. The custodian holds them. Private equity and private credit firms do deals away from any market. And rating agencies and auditors tell everyone else what is safe and what the accounts say.<br><br>For each one I give the job in a sentence, the way it is paid, and the names of the largest. Pay attention to how each is paid, and by whom. Several are paid by the party they are supposed to be judging or bargaining against, and that fact comes back in Part 3.",
 bridge="The six slides follow a pension contribution inward. The first hands it to an asset manager. The second shows the investment bank that made the bond the manager buys. The third shows the broker, exchange and market maker through which it is bought. The fourth shows the custodian that holds it. The fifth turns to private equity and private credit. The sixth is the graders and checkers.",
 out="Six kinds of middle firm, each taking a fee or a spread from the same pool of savings. One piece of the cast is still missing: the referees.",
 rec="Asset managers, investment banks, brokers and exchanges, custodians, private funds and raters all work between the saver and the borrower.",
 roles=[],
 src=[S("Thinking Ahead Institute and Pensions & Investments, The world's largest 500 asset managers (2025)", TAIU),
      S("Global Custodian, A new era of custody (2025)", GC),
      S("US Securities and Exchange Commission, staff report on nationally recognized statistical rating organizations (January 2025)", SECR),
      S("Financial Stability Board, Global Monitoring Report on Nonbank Financial Intermediation 2025", FSBR),
      S("World Bank, Global Financial Development Database: mutual fund assets to GDP", WBI + "GFDD.DI.07")],
 steps=[
  st("End of 2024", "New York", NY, 3.2, "The Asset Manager: Paid a Slice Every Year",
   "An asset manager invests money for others: pension funds, insurers, governments and ordinary savers. It pools the money into funds. An active fund pays people to pick investments. An index fund simply buys every company on a published list, such as the 500 largest American companies. The manager is paid a percentage of the money each year, whether the fund rises or falls. On 100,000 of savings, a 1 per cent fee is 1,000 a year, taken before the saver sees any return. The 500 largest managers held 139.9 trillion dollars at the end of 2024. The largest, BlackRock, held 11.55 trillion and the second, Vanguard, 10.1 trillion. The rule to remember: the manager's income depends on the size of the pile, so its first interest is to gather more money, and only its second is how that money performs.",
   TAIU, pl=["blackrock", "vanguard", "fidelity", "capital", "amundi", "pimco"], ex=["tai2025"]),
  st("Today", "New York and London", [-37, 46], 2.3, "The Investment Bank: Maker and Seller of Securities",
   "An investment bank takes no deposits from the public. It does three things. It underwrites: when a company or government wants to raise money, the bank designs the bond or share, finds the buyers and takes a cut of the sum raised. It advises on takeovers, for a fee that is paid when the deal is done. And it trades, buying and selling for clients and for itself. A security is the general word for a bond, a share or anything else that can be bought and sold like them. Since the 1990s the largest banks have done all of this under one roof with ordinary banking, so the same firm can lend to a company, sell its shares, advise its rival and trade against its own customers. The rule: an underwriter is paid when the sale goes through, not when the buyer does well out of it.",
   "https://www.fsb.org/2025/11/2025-list-of-global-systemically-important-banks-g-sibs/", pl=["gs", "ms", "jpm", "bofa", "citi", "barclays", "deutsche", "ubs", "nomura"], arcs=[[NY, LONDON]], an=[["New York", "London"]]),
  st("Today", "Chicago to Frankfurt", [-40, 45], 2.1, "The Broker, the Exchange and the Market Maker",
   "You cannot walk into a stock exchange. A broker takes your order and passes it on, for a commission or, increasingly, for a payment from the firm it sends the order to. An exchange is the place, now a computer, where orders meet. Nearly every large exchange is itself a company run for profit, and it earns from trading fees, from selling price data and from the clearing houses it owns. A market maker stands in the middle all day, offering to buy at 9.99 and sell at 10.01, and keeps the 2 cents. This map treats markets only lightly; my map of the stock market goes further. What matters here is the rule: all three earn more when there is more trading, whether or not the trading does the saver any good.",
   "https://www.fsb.org/uploads/P161225.pdf", pl=["ice", "nasdaq", "cme", "lseg", "dboerse", "euronext", "schwab", "citadelsec", "virtu"], note="The ringed circles are six exchange groups, one retail broker and two market makers named as examples, each at its headquarters. Every exchange recorded in Wikidata is in the dots layer."),
  st("Mid-2026", "New York and Boston", [-73, 41.6], 5.2, "The Custodian: The Firm That Actually Holds It",
   "When a pension fund buys a share, it does not keep it. A custodian bank holds it, collects the dividends, votes or passes on the vote, and reports what is there. The fee is tiny, a few hundredths of a per cent, so the business only pays at enormous scale, and four banks have nearly all of it. In October 2025 the trade paper Global Custodian put the securities held by BNY, State Street, JPMorgan and Citi at 180 trillion dollars combined, more than the world produces in a year. By mid-2026 BNY alone reported 62.6 trillion and State Street 58 trillion. The rule: if you own a fund, your name appears on no share. The fund's name is on the custodian's books, and the custodian's name is on the books of a depository. Chapter 6 follows that chain to the end.",
   GC, pl=["bny", "statestreet", "jpm", "citi", "northerntrust", "bbh"], ex=["custody2025"]),
  st("End of 2024", "New York", NY, 3.2, "Private Equity and Private Credit: Deals Off the Market",
   "A private equity firm raises a fund from pension funds, insurers and state funds, borrows several times as much again, and buys whole companies. The borrowed money becomes the debt of the company that was bought, not of the firm that bought it. The firm charges about 2 per cent of the fund each year and keeps about a fifth of any gain. Private credit is the lending version: a fund lends directly to a company, doing what a bank would once have done, without a bank's rules. Neither has to publish what a listed company or a bank must publish. The Financial Stability Board said in December 2025 that data on private credit was so limited that countries could not even agree on a definition. The rule: if the bought company fails, its workers and lenders lose; the fees have already been paid.",
   FSBN, pl=["blackstone", "kkr", "apollo", "carlyle", "brookfield", "ares", "eqt", "cvc", "blueowl", "tpg"]),
  st("End of 2023", "New York and London", [-37, 46], 2.3, "The Graders and the Checkers",
   "Two kinds of firm tell everyone else what to believe. A credit rating agency gives a borrower a grade, from AAA at the top down to D for default, and many funds are only allowed to buy bonds above a certain grade. An auditor checks a company's accounts and signs them. In both cases the firm being judged chooses the judge and pays the bill. Three agencies, S&P, Moody's and Fitch, issued 94.15 per cent of all ratings outstanding at the end of 2023 from agencies registered in the United States. Four accounting networks audit nearly every large listed company. A third kind of firm, the index maker, decides which companies and countries go on the lists that index funds must copy. The rule: a grader paid by the graded loses business when it is strict.",
   SECR, pl=["sp", "moodys", "fitch", "deloitte", "pwc", "ey", "kpmg", "msci"], ex=["sec_nrsro"], arcs=[[NY, LONDON]], an=[["New York", "London"]]),
 ])

flow(id="referees", part=0, scope="world",
 title="The Referees",
 kick="Every country has public bodies that license and watch these firms. Who they are, and who pays them, differs more than most people expect.",
 sum="The cast has one more group. Because a bank can fail in a day and take its customers' money with it, every country licenses financial firms and appoints someone to watch them. These bodies are the referees.<br><br>They come in three kinds, and it helps to keep them apart. A prudential regulator watches whether a firm is safe: does the bank have enough of its own money to absorb losses, can the insurer pay its claims. A conduct regulator watches how firms treat customers and whether markets are honest. A deposit insurer repays savers, up to a limit, when a bank fails. Some countries put all three in one body. The United States spreads them across more than half a dozen.<br><br>This chapter only introduces them. Part 3 looks at who writes the rules they enforce.",
 bridge="The two slides are short. The first sorts the referees into their three kinds and shows where the main ones sit. The second explains the one safety measure every later chapter relies on: the rule that a bank must fund part of itself with its owners' money.",
 out="The cast is complete: the takers, the middle firms and the referees. Part 2 sets them in motion.",
 rec="Regulators check that firms are safe and honest, and deposit insurers repay savers up to a limit when a bank fails.",
 roles=[],
 src=[S("Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, About the OCC", OCCU),
      S("Basel Committee on Banking Supervision, charter", "https://www.bis.org/bcbs/charter.htm"),
      S("Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, joint statement of 12 March 2023", "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2023/pr23017.html")],
 steps=[
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "Three Kinds of Referee",
   "Sort the referees by what they watch. Safety: in the United States the Comptroller of the Currency, the Federal Reserve and the deposit insurer share the banks; in the euro area the largest banks answer to the European Central Bank; in Britain to an arm of the Bank of England. Conduct and markets: the Securities and Exchange Commission in Washington, the Financial Conduct Authority in London, and their equivalents elsewhere. Deposit insurance: in the United States a depositor is covered up to 250,000 dollars per bank. Then ask who pays the referee. Many are funded by the firms they watch. The Comptroller says its budget is funded primarily by assessments and fees paid by banks. If a bank can choose which referee licenses it, the referee has a reason to keep that bank content.",
   OCCU, w=1, pl=["sec", "cftc", "occreg", "fdic", "fca", "pra", "ssm", "esma", "bafin", "finma", "jfsa", "nfra", "csrc", "sebi", "mas", "apra", "osfi", "cvm", "fsca"], ex=["occ_funding"], note="The ringed circles are national regulators from the directory, each at its headquarters city. The directory lists %(n_reg)s of them; it is weighted toward the largest financial centres and is not a complete list of the world's regulators."),
  st("Today", "Basel", BASEL, 3.4, "The One Rule Everything Else Leans On: Capital",
   "A bank funds its loans with two kinds of money: money it owes, mostly deposits, and money that belongs to its owners, called capital. Take a bank with 100 of loans, 95 of deposits and 5 of capital. If loans worth 3 go bad, the owners absorb the loss and depositors are whole. If loans worth 6 go bad, the capital is gone and the bank owes more than it has. So the central rule of bank regulation is a minimum amount of capital. Now the incentive. On the same 100 of loans, the owners' profit is spread over 5 of their own money instead of 10, so their return doubles when capital is halved. Owners therefore want as little capital as the rule allows, and the public wants more. The body that sets the world's minimum sits in Basel, and Chapter 9 goes there.",
   "https://www.bis.org/bcbs/charter.htm", pl=["bcbs"], note="The ringed circle is the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision. The bank and its numbers are a worked example I wrote to show the rule."),
 ])

# ================================================================== PART 2 · THE MOTION
flow(id="payment", part=1, scope="world",
 title="One Payment, Followed",
 kick="Paying someone looks instant. Behind the screen a payment is two separate things, a message and a movement of money, and they travel by different roads.",
 sum="Part 1 was a list. Part 2 is motion. I follow three things through the system from start to finish: a payment in this chapter, a share in the next, and an overnight loan in the one after. The last chapter of the part steps back to show where all the money ends up.<br><br>Start with the idea that explains every payment. Money in a bank account is an entry in that bank's records. To pay someone is to lower one entry and raise another. If both people use the same bank, the bank edits two lines and nothing leaves the building. If they use different banks, one bank now owes the other, and that debt has to be settled with money both banks accept. If they are in different countries with different currencies, no single body's records cover both, and a chain of banks has to be built.<br><br>Two words are used throughout. Clearing means working out who owes what to whom. Settlement means actually paying it, after which the matter is finished and cannot be undone.",
 bridge="The five slides walk outward. The first is a payment inside one bank. The second is between two banks in one country, where the central bank's books are used. The third is a card payment and the fee inside it. The fourth crosses a border through a chain of correspondent banks. The fifth is a currency trade and the system built to stop one side paying for nothing.",
 out="A payment is a message plus a settlement. Inside a country it settles on the central bank's books; across borders it rides a chain of private banks. Next, a share.",
 rec="A payment is a message plus a movement of money; across borders the money moves through a chain of correspondent banks.",
 roles=R3,
 src=[S("Federal Reserve Financial Services, Fedwire Funds Service annual statistics", FEDW), S("The Clearing House, CHIPS", CHIPSU),
      S("Wikipedia, Interchange fee (United States average and European Union caps)", INTW),
      S("Bank for International Settlements, CPMI correspondent banking data", CPMIC), S("Swift, Who we are", SWIFTW),
      S("CLS Group, About us", CLSU), S("Bank for International Settlements, Triennial Central Bank Survey 2025", BISFX)],
 steps=[
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "Same Bank: Two Lines in One Book",
   "Ana pays Ben 50, and both bank at the same bank. The bank lowers Ana's balance by 50 and raises Ben's by 50. No coin or note moves, and no other institution is involved. This is the cheapest payment there is, and it explains why a bank wants as many customers as possible: the more people on its books, the more payments it can complete alone. It is also why phone wallets such as M-Pesa in Kenya or Alipay in China could grow so fast. Each is one very large book. The rule: if payer and receiver are on the same book, a payment is only an edit.",
   FEDW, w=1, pl=["mpesa", "ant", "tenpay"], note="This slide shades no country. The ringed circles are three phone-wallet operators named in the text."),
  st("2025", "New York and Washington", [-75.5, 39.8], 5.0, "Two Banks: Settling on the Central Bank's Books",
   "Now Ana banks at Bank A and Ben at Bank B. Bank A lowers Ana's balance, Bank B raises Ben's, and Bank A owes Bank B 50. Banks settle such debts across accounts they all hold at the central bank. There are two ways. One at a time and at once: the United States system for this, Fedwire, moved an average of 4.59 trillion dollars a day in 2025. Or by netting: add up everything the banks owe each other over a period and pay only the differences. If A owes B 50 and B owes A 45, a single payment of 5 settles both. The privately owned system CHIPS works this way and says each dollar it holds settles about 26 dollars of payments, more than 2 trillion a day. The rule: netting saves money, but until the net payment is made, every bank is exposed to every other.",
   FEDW, pl=["fedwire", "tch"], arcs=[[NY, WASH]], an=[["New York", "Washington"]], ex=["fedwire2025"]),
  st("Today", "San Francisco and New York", [-98, 39], 2.6, "A Card Payment: Four Parties and a Fee Inside the Price",
   "Ana pays a shop 100 by card. Four parties act. Her bank, which issued the card. The shop's bank. The card network, usually Visa or Mastercard, which connects them. And the shop. The shop does not receive 100. Its bank passes on about 98, and most of the missing 2 goes to Ana's bank. That part is called the interchange fee, and the network sets it. In the United States it averages about 2 per cent of the sale. The European Union capped it in 2015 at 0.3 per cent for credit cards and 0.2 per cent for debit cards. Shops build the fee into their prices, so a customer paying cash pays it too. The rule: if the party that sets a fee is not the party that pays it, the fee has no natural ceiling.",
   INTW, pl=["visa", "mastercard", "amex", "unionpay", "jcb"], arcs=[[SF, NY]], an=[["San Francisco", "New York"]]),
  st("Today", "Nairobi to New York to Bangkok", [20, 22], 1.7, "Across a Border: A Chain of Other Banks",
   "A Kenyan importer pays a Thai exporter 10,000 dollars. The Kenyan bank has no account with the Thai bank and neither has an account at the United States central bank. Each instead keeps a dollar account with a large bank in New York, called its correspondent. The Kenyan bank sends an instruction over Swift, a messaging network; Swift carries the words, not the money. Its New York correspondent pays the Thai bank's New York correspondent through CHIPS, and that bank credits the Thai bank. So a payment between Nairobi and Bangkok settles in New York, under United States law. The Bank for International Settlements describes this network as shrinking and becoming more concentrated. The rule: if the large bank in the middle closes your bank's account, your country's payments in that currency stop.",
   CPMIC, add={"Kenya": 0, "United States": 1, "Thailand": 2}, arcs=[[NAIROBI, NY], [NY, BANGKOK]], an=[["Nairobi", "New York"], ["New York", "Bangkok"]], pl=["swift", "tch", "citi", "jpm", "stanchart"], ex=["cpmi_corr"]),
  st("April 2025", "London, New York, Tokyo", [15, 42], 1.6, "Swapping Currencies: Paying Without Receiving",
   "Most payments across borders also need one currency exchanged for another. In April 2025 currency trading averaged 9.6 trillion dollars a day, and the dollar was on one side of 89 per cent of trades. A currency trade has two halves. A bank in Tokyo pays yen in the morning there, and waits for dollars that will only arrive when New York opens. If the other bank fails in between, the yen are gone and the dollars never come. A German bank, Herstatt, failed in exactly that gap in 1974. Since 2002 a bank owned by the big trading banks, CLS, has settled both halves at the same instant. It handles more than 8 trillion dollars on an average day in 18 currencies. The rule: currencies outside those 18 still settle the old way, with the risk left in.",
   CLSU, add={"Japan": 0, "United Kingdom": 1, "United States": 2}, arcs=[[TOKYO, LONDON], [LONDON, NY]], an=[["Tokyo", "London"], ["London", "New York"]], pl=["cls"], ex=["cls_about"]),
 ])

flow(id="share", part=1, scope="world",
 title="One Share, Followed",
 kick="Buying a share takes a second on a phone. Finishing the purchase takes a day and four institutions, and at the end your name is not on the share.",
 sum="A payment moved money. A trade moves two things in opposite directions, money one way and a security the other, and both sides worry that they will hand over theirs and get nothing back. Most of the machinery in this chapter exists to remove that worry.<br><br>Three steps happen after you press buy. The trade is made: your order meets a seller's. The trade is cleared: a clearing house steps between buyer and seller and guarantees both. The trade is settled: the share and the money change hands for good, in most large markets now one working day later. Each step has its own institution, and most countries have exactly one of each.<br><br>This is the part of finance usually called the plumbing. Almost nobody outside it knows the names, and the sums that pass through are the largest in the system.",
 bridge="The four slides follow one order. The first takes it from a phone to the market. The second shows the clearing house stepping into the middle. The third shows settlement at the depository and the sum that passes through. The fourth asks whose name is on the share at the end.",
 out="A trade is made on a market, guaranteed by a clearing house and settled at a depository, and the investor ends up with a claim on a chain of intermediaries. Next, the loan that keeps this running overnight.",
 rec="A trade is made, cleared and settled by three different institutions, and the share ends up registered in a depository's name.",
 roles=R3,
 src=[S("DTCC, 2024 annual report: delivering value", DTCCV), S("DTCC, press release of 18 June 2025", DTC100),
      S("Wikipedia, Cede and Company", CEDE), S("Wikipedia, Central counterparty clearing", CCPW)],
 steps=[
  st("Today", "New York", NY, 3.0, "The Order: From a Phone to the Market",
   "Ana buys 10 shares of a company at 20 each on her broker's app. The broker does not own a stock exchange. It sends her order to an exchange or, very often for small orders in the United States, to a market maker that pays the broker for the right to fill it. Within a fraction of a second her order is matched with a seller's. At that moment a trade exists: a promise that she will pay 200 and receive 10 shares. Nothing has yet changed hands. Her app shows the shares at once, but that display is the broker's own record. The rule: a trade is a promise, and a promise is only as good as the party on the other side, whom Ana will never know.",
   DTCCV, pl=["robinhood", "schwab", "citadelsec", "virtu", "ice", "nasdaq"], add={"United States": 0}),
  st("Since 2009", "London, Chicago, Frankfurt", [-40, 46], 2.1, "The Clearing House: Buyer to Every Seller",
   "Between the trade and the settlement, either side might fail. A clearing house removes that risk by replacing the one promise with two. It becomes the buyer to the seller and the seller to the buyer. Now neither depends on the other, only on the clearing house. To protect itself it makes each member post margin, which is cash or bonds handed over in advance as a deposit against losses, and it tops this up every day as prices move. Members also pay into a shared fund that is used if one of them fails. After the crash of 2008 the leaders of the twenty largest economies agreed that standard derivatives too must go through clearing houses. The rule: clearing does not destroy risk. It collects it from thousands of firms and places it in one.",
   CCPW, pl=["lch", "cme", "eurexclearing", "iceclearcredit", "occ", "jscc", "dtcc"], arcs=[[CHICAGO, LONDON], [LONDON, FRANKFURT]], an=[["Chicago", "London"], ["London", "Frankfurt"]]),
  st("2024", "New York", NY, 3.0, "Settlement: One Depository Per Country",
   "The next working day, Ana's trade settles. No paper moves. The shares of nearly every American company sit, in electronic form, at one depository in New York, the Depository Trust Company. Settlement is an edit to its records: 10 shares leave the account of the seller's broker and enter the account of Ana's broker, and 200 dollars moves the other way. Brokers' trades are netted first, so that only the differences move. The depository's parent, DTCC, is owned by the banks and brokers that use it. It processed 3.79 quadrillion dollars of securities transactions in 2024, which is 3,790 trillion. In June 2025 the depository said it held more than 100 trillion dollars of securities. Most countries have one such body. The rule: if it stops for a day, no share or bond in that country can change owner.",
   DTCCV, pl=["dtcc", "euroclear", "clearstream", "csdc", "jasdec", "nsdl", "ksd", "strate"], ex=["dtcc2024"], add={"United States": 1}, note="The ringed circles are the depositories of eight markets, from the directory. The lightly shaded country is the United States, whose depository the slide describes."),
  st("Today", "New York", NY, 3.0, "Whose Name Is on Ana's Share",
   "Ana paid for 10 shares. The company's register of owners does not show her name. It does not show her broker's name either. Securities held at the Depository Trust Company are registered in the name of Cede & Co., a nominee, which means a name used to hold property for others. In the last year the depository published the share, 1998, Cede was the registered owner of 83 per cent of all shares issued in the United States. The depository's books show Ana's broker. The broker's books show Ana. What she owns is a claim on her broker, which has a claim on the depository. This is how nearly everyone holds securities, and in normal times it changes nothing. The rule: if a link in that chain fails or is frozen, the investor has to prove a claim, not collect a possession.",
   CEDE, pl=["dtcc"], ex=["cede"], add={"United States": 2}),
 ])

flow(id="repo", part=1, scope="world",
 title="Overnight: Repo, Collateral and Margin",
 kick="The large firms in the last two chapters fund themselves with loans that last one night, renewed every morning. The loans are safe as long as the collateral holds its price.",
 sum="A market maker holds billions in bonds it bought an hour ago and hopes to sell tomorrow. It did not pay for them with its own money. It borrowed the money overnight, and it will borrow it again tomorrow night. The loan it uses is called repo, short for repurchase agreement, and it is the least known large market in the world.<br><br>Repo is a loan dressed as a sale. The borrower sells a bond today and agrees to buy it back tomorrow for slightly more. The bond is the lender's security: if the borrower fails, the lender keeps it. Anything handed over as security for a loan is called collateral. Because good collateral makes the loan nearly riskless, cash-rich firms such as money market funds lend this way in enormous amounts.<br><br>I include this chapter because repo is where trouble spreads fastest. Minor overlap aside, I leave the wider subject of lending outside banks to the page that covers it. Here the point is the mechanism, which is the same one behind the margin a clearing house demands.",
 bridge="The four slides build one chain of cause and effect. The first sets up a single overnight loan with numbers. The second shows who sits in the middle of the market. The third shows what happens when the collateral falls in price. The fourth is a case where exactly that happened to pension funds.",
 out="Overnight loans against collateral hold the trading system up, and a fall in the collateral's price forces sales that push the price down further. One chapter of motion remains: where all the money ends up.",
 rec="Firms fund their holdings overnight against collateral, and a falling price forces them to find cash or sell.",
 roles=R3,
 src=[S("Federal Reserve Bank of New York, Tri-party and GCF repo statistics", TRI), S("Federal Reserve Bank of New York, Secured Overnight Financing Rate", SOFR),
      S("Bank of England, announcement of gilt market operation, 28 September 2022", BOEG)],
 steps=[
  st("Today", "New York", NY, 3.0, "One Night's Loan, With Numbers",
   "A dealer owns a government bond worth 100 and needs cash. A money market fund has cash it wants back tomorrow with a little interest. The dealer sells the bond to the fund for 98 tonight and agrees to buy it back tomorrow for 98.01. The 2 held back is called the haircut: a cushion for the lender in case the bond's price falls. In effect the dealer has borrowed 98 for a night at a small rate, and the fund holds a bond worth more than its loan. Tomorrow they do it again. A dealer can hold a 100 bond with only 2 of its own money this way. With 2 of its own money and a string of such loans, it can hold fifty times that. Borrowing to hold more than you could buy outright is called leverage.",
   SOFR, add={"United States": 0}, note="The loan and its numbers are a worked example I wrote to show the rule. The United States is shaded because the next slides describe its market."),
  st("Today", "New York", NY, 3.0, "The Firm in the Middle of the Market",
   "The dealer and the fund do not swap bonds and cash by hand. In the largest part of the American market a third firm stands between them, holds the collateral, checks its value each day and moves the cash. This is called tri-party repo. The Federal Reserve Bank of New York describes it as a large market that provides short-term funding for securities dealers, and it collects the market's data from one bank, BNY. The rate paid in this market also became the base for other prices: since 2018 the Federal Reserve Bank of New York has published an average overnight repo rate, called SOFR, which replaced the scandal-hit Libor as the reference for dollar loans and derivatives. The rule: the price of one night's money in this market feeds into what most other dollar borrowing costs.",
   TRI, pl=["bny", "jpm", "gs", "ms"], add={"United States": 1}),
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "If the Collateral Falls: The Margin Call",
   "Go back to the dealer with a 100 bond funded by a 98 loan. Overnight the bond's price falls to 95. The lender will now lend only 93.10 against it, keeping the same haircut. The dealer must find 4.90 in cash by morning. That demand is a margin call. If the dealer has the cash, nothing more happens. If it does not, it must sell bonds. If many dealers and funds hold the same bonds and get the same call on the same morning, they all sell together, the price falls again, and the next morning's calls are larger. Follow the chain: lower price, then margin call, then forced sale, then lower price. Clearing houses run on the same daily calls. The rule: a system built on collateral is stable only while prices are, and it turns a price fall into a scramble for cash.",
   TRI, w=1, note="This slide shades no country. The numbers continue the worked example from the first slide of the chapter."),
  st("September 2022", "London", LONDON, 3.6, "Regional Example: British Pension Funds and the Bond Sell-Off",
   "Regional Example: British pension funds and the bond sell-off of 2022. Many British pension funds that had promised set incomes had used borrowed money and derivatives, backed by government bonds as collateral, to match what they owed. In late September 2022 the price of long-dated British government bonds fell sharply within days. The funds received margin calls, sold bonds to raise cash, and the sales pushed prices lower. On 28 September the Bank of England said that if dysfunction in this market continued there would be a material risk to the country's financial stability. It began buying long-dated government bonds, on whatever scale was necessary, with the Treasury covering any loss. The funds involved were among the most cautious investors in the country. The mechanism from the last slide reached them anyway, and public money stopped it.",
   BOEG, reg=True, add={"United Kingdom": 2}, ex=["boe_gilt"], pl=["pra"]),
 ])

flow(id="flowoffunds", part=1, scope="world",
 title="Where the Money Ends Up",
 kick="Follow the savings through every pipe in the last three chapters and ask what they finally pay for. The answer is mostly not new businesses.",
 sum="The textbook account of finance is simple. Households save. The financial system collects the savings and lends them to businesses, which build factories and hire people. Economists call the full account of who lends to whom the flow of funds.<br><br>The parts and pipes described so far could do that job. This chapter looks at what they do instead. Three things stand out when the lending is added up. Most bank lending goes to buy property that already exists. A growing share of financial activity is one financial firm dealing with another. And the route from a saver to a company has become so long that each pound, dollar or rupee passes through many hands, each of which takes a little.<br><br>This is still description. But it is the description that Part 4 puts a price on.",
 bridge="The three slides trace the loop. The first shows what banks in rich countries lend for, over more than a century. The second maps how much private borrowing each country carries. The third lays the saver's chain end to end and counts the hands.",
 out="Savings mostly finance property and other finance, and they pass through a long chain on the way. Part 3 asks who is in charge of that chain.",
 rec="Most bank lending now goes to property, and savings pass through a long chain of firms before reaching any borrower.",
 roles=[],
 src=[S("Jordà, Schularick and Taylor, The Great Mortgaging (Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco Economic Letter, 2015)", JST),
      S("Jordà, Schularick and Taylor, The great mortgaging (VoxEU, 2014)", JSTV),
      S("World Bank, Domestic credit to private sector (per cent of GDP)", WBI + "FS.AST.PRVT.GD.ZS")],
 steps=[
  st("1900 to 2014", "Seventeen rich countries", [-30, 45], 1.9, "What Banks Lend For",
   "Three economists, Jordà, Schularick and Taylor, collected the records of bank lending in 17 rich countries back to 1870. In 1900 about 30 per cent of bank loans were mortgages, which are loans secured on property. By 2014 the share was about 60 per cent. Over the same decades total bank lending grew from about 30 per cent of GDP in 1945 to about 120 per cent just before 2008. They wrote that the core business of banks in rich countries now resembles that of a property fund, and that lending household savings to business, the textbook job, is a much smaller part of banking than it was. When banks create loans against houses that already exist, no new house is built by the loan. The price of the existing ones rises, and so does the debt of whoever buys next.",
   JST, ex=["jst"], w=0),
  st("Latest year", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "How Much Private Debt Each Country Carries",
   "The map shades each country by the debts of its households and businesses to financial firms, measured against GDP. It is the other side of the savings in Part 1: every sum saved in the system is a sum someone else owes. In %(credit_100)s of %(credit_n)s countries private debt is larger than a year's output. In %(credit_low)s it is under 30 per cent. A high figure does not show that a country invests a lot. As the last slide showed, in rich countries most of it is borrowing against property. A very low figure usually means something different: that most people and small businesses there cannot borrow from the formal system at all, and rely on family, traders or moneylenders at far higher rates.",
   WBI + "FS.AST.PRVT.GD.ZS", data={"k": "credit"}),
  st("Today", "Boston to New York", [-73, 41.6], 5.0, "The Chain From a Saver to a Company",
   "Count the hands between a worker and a company her pension owns. Her employer pays into a pension fund. The fund's trustees hire a consultant to choose managers. The consultant recommends an asset manager. The manager puts the money in a fund it runs. The fund's orders go through a broker to an exchange or a market maker. A clearing house guarantees the trade and a depository settles it. A custodian holds the shares, and may lend them out to other traders for a fee. An index maker decided which shares the fund had to buy, and a rating agency graded its bonds. That is at least ten firms. Each is paid a fraction of a per cent. None of the payments is large enough to notice, and the worker is sent a bill for none of them. They come out of the pension.",
   TAIU, pl=["calpers", "blackrock", "statestreet", "bny", "dtcc", "ice", "citadelsec", "msci", "sp"], note="The ringed circles are one example of each link in the chain, from the directory. They were chosen to show the links, and are not the only firms in each role."),
 ])
